WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.216 --> 00:00:02.831
- I do not want to be a one and done.

00:00:02.831 --> 00:00:05.991
I do not want to be a flash in the pan.

00:00:05.991 --> 00:00:08.153
I want to be here for the long haul.

00:00:08.153 --> 00:00:09.421
You got to make a way.

00:00:09.421 --> 00:00:10.500
When there doesn't seem to be a way,

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:11.618
you got to make a way.

00:00:11.618 --> 00:00:15.988
You have to bust your
ass, roll up your sleeves,

00:00:15.988 --> 00:00:20.957
and attack, attack, attack,
attack every single day.

00:00:20.957 --> 00:00:22.455
- He's an American film director,

00:00:22.455 --> 00:00:24.530
producer, writer and actor.

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:26.863
His production company,
40 Acres And A Mule,

00:00:26.863 --> 00:00:30.348
have produced over 35 films since 1983.

00:00:30.348 --> 00:00:33.173
He's won numerous awards
including an Emmy Award.

00:00:33.173 --> 00:00:34.827
He's Spike Lee and here's my take

00:00:34.827 --> 00:00:36.691
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:36.691 --> 00:00:38.374
Rule number six is my personal favorite.

00:00:38.374 --> 00:00:40.156
And make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:40.156 --> 00:00:41.735
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:41.735 --> 00:00:43.345
And as always, if Spike says something

00:00:43.345 --> 00:00:46.203
that's really meaningful and
relevant and inspiring to you,

00:00:46.203 --> 00:00:47.575
please leave it in the comments below.

00:00:47.575 --> 00:00:49.237
And put quotes around it so other people

00:00:49.237 --> 00:00:51.177
can be inspired as well.

00:00:51.177 --> 00:00:52.010
Enjoy!

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.910
(dramatic music)

00:01:04.976 --> 00:01:08.838
- I did not become a filmmaker
'cause I want to be famous.

00:01:08.838 --> 00:01:10.165
I did not become a filmmaker

00:01:10.165 --> 00:01:13.082
'cause I want to make a lot of money.

00:01:13.978 --> 00:01:16.888
I became a filmmaker 'cause
I found something I love.

00:01:16.888 --> 00:01:19.049
I'm a professor at NYU,

00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:20.817
the graduate of film
school, been here 15 years.

00:01:20.817 --> 00:01:23.558
I'm also a director, artistic
director of the film school.

00:01:23.558 --> 00:01:25.140
I tell to all my students,

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:28.052
the worst thing you'd
do is choose your major

00:01:28.052 --> 00:01:31.006
just based solely on how much money

00:01:31.006 --> 00:01:34.098
you're thinking to make when you graduate.

00:01:34.098 --> 00:01:37.288
If you want to be happy, have a happy life

00:01:37.288 --> 00:01:39.320
and not be miserable.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.097
Find something that you love.

00:01:42.097 --> 00:01:43.136
That's it.

00:01:43.136 --> 00:01:47.711
Laziness is something I
really can't tolerate.

00:01:47.711 --> 00:01:49.628
So because of laziness,

00:01:50.576 --> 00:01:54.743
I think that that speaks of
a sense of entitlement and,

00:02:00.098 --> 00:02:02.836
that's just not a good
look in this industry

00:02:02.836 --> 00:02:06.593
where you think that you're
entitled to something,

00:02:06.593 --> 00:02:09.756
that you deserve something because

00:02:09.756 --> 00:02:12.255
you are whatever you think you are.

00:02:12.255 --> 00:02:13.379
It doesn't work like that.

00:02:13.379 --> 00:02:15.546
And another thing I'd like to add is that,

00:02:15.546 --> 00:02:17.175
and I'm speaking about my school,

00:02:17.175 --> 00:02:19.425
a lot of people come on NYU

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.270
thinking that because you have this degree

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.917
from this prestigious,
prestigious institution,

00:02:26.917 --> 00:02:29.417
NYU or USC or AFI or Columbia.

00:02:32.398 --> 00:02:33.981
In the real world,

00:02:33.981 --> 00:02:36.190
they don't really give a sh.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.607
If you don't have the talent,

00:02:39.483 --> 00:02:41.808
that degree doesn't mean a thing.

00:02:41.808 --> 00:02:44.735
When you come out of
school, you should have,

00:02:44.735 --> 00:02:46.230
if you're a director
you should have a film,

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.763
if you're a DP you should have a reel,

00:02:48.763 --> 00:02:51.013
editors should have a reel.

00:02:52.625 --> 00:02:55.662
If you're a screen writer,
you should have scripts.

00:02:55.662 --> 00:02:59.662
So people really want to
see the work you've done.

00:03:01.921 --> 00:03:06.434
I came to the mindset when
I was in film school, Marc,

00:03:06.434 --> 00:03:09.222
that I do not want to be a one and done,

00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:12.253
I do not want to be a flash in the pan.

00:03:12.253 --> 00:03:14.493
I want to be here for the long haul.

00:03:14.493 --> 00:03:16.941
And when Kurosawa, the
great Akira Kurosawa

00:03:16.941 --> 00:03:21.174
was doing press for one
of his last films, Ran,

00:03:21.174 --> 00:03:23.669
these journalists asked him,

00:03:23.669 --> 00:03:27.852
you're one of the greatest
master filmmakers of all time,

00:03:27.852 --> 00:03:30.016
what else do you still have to learn?

00:03:30.016 --> 00:03:34.077
And Kurosawa said I still
have the universe to learn.

00:03:34.077 --> 00:03:38.183
So I was in film school
and that blew my mind.

00:03:38.183 --> 00:03:39.310
The baddest ever.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:40.143
- Right.

00:03:40.143 --> 00:03:41.643
- He's 85, 83, 84.

00:03:42.638 --> 00:03:44.591
And he says he still has
the universe to learn

00:03:44.591 --> 00:03:46.518
as far as storytelling.

00:03:46.518 --> 00:03:47.726
That really hit me.

00:03:47.726 --> 00:03:49.953
You really got to, like the jazz term,

00:03:49.953 --> 00:03:51.578
you got to stay in the woodshed,

00:03:51.578 --> 00:03:52.822
you got to shed,

00:03:52.822 --> 00:03:55.351
and constantly be on it,

00:03:55.351 --> 00:03:58.711
perfecting your craft and
never get to the point

00:03:58.711 --> 00:04:01.196
that you trick yourself of
thinking that you know it all.

00:04:01.196 --> 00:04:02.107
- Yeah.

00:04:02.107 --> 00:04:04.357
- It's a life long journey.

00:04:06.386 --> 00:04:10.453
the stories, to tell
stories, how you craft them.

00:04:10.453 --> 00:04:11.575
Just be a good storytelling

00:04:11.575 --> 00:04:14.977
and you do that by having a body of work.

00:04:14.977 --> 00:04:17.298
So it's always been on the body of work.

00:04:17.298 --> 00:04:19.287
The reason why I've told
the story I've told,

00:04:19.287 --> 00:04:21.577
the story I want to tell
at that particular time,

00:04:21.577 --> 00:04:23.454
it's part of being a human being.

00:04:23.454 --> 00:04:26.645
It's something that makes us who we are.

00:04:26.645 --> 00:04:29.510
My favorite filmmakers are storytellers.

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:30.807
And that's what I try to do.

00:04:30.807 --> 00:04:34.974
It allows me to express myself
and share it with the world.

00:04:36.162 --> 00:04:37.540
Everybody comes into the theater

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:41.033
and sits down in a chair in the dark room.

00:04:41.033 --> 00:04:45.884
Who they are is going to
affect how they view the film

00:04:45.884 --> 00:04:46.962
and how they receive it.

00:04:46.962 --> 00:04:48.418
You want them to have some feeling.

00:04:48.418 --> 00:04:50.619
About what that feeling is,

00:04:50.619 --> 00:04:53.658
that depends on the person's,

00:04:53.658 --> 00:04:56.870
who they are when they experience it.

00:04:56.870 --> 00:04:58.951
You make a market.

00:04:58.951 --> 00:05:02.161
Was there a market for
She's Got to Have It?

00:05:02.161 --> 00:05:04.328
You make it, you have to.

00:05:06.902 --> 00:05:08.860
There was no market for hip-hop
before there was hip-hop.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.689
Some guys in the Bronx
started getting together,

00:05:10.689 --> 00:05:12.433
rapping all the beats in.

00:05:12.433 --> 00:05:15.999
Now it's a billion-dollar industry, so.

00:05:15.999 --> 00:05:17.374
You got to make a way.

00:05:17.374 --> 00:05:18.447
And when there doesn't seem to be the way,

00:05:18.447 --> 00:05:19.564
you got to make away.

00:05:19.564 --> 00:05:23.291
One of my favorite words is visionary

00:05:23.291 --> 00:05:24.656
because these are the people,

00:05:24.656 --> 00:05:27.356
visionaries are people who see something

00:05:27.356 --> 00:05:30.057
when no one else can see it.

00:05:30.057 --> 00:05:31.885
And when you can see something
when no one can else can see,

00:05:31.885 --> 00:05:34.802
you're called crazy but you see it.

00:05:35.799 --> 00:05:37.382
And you're ahead of other people

00:05:37.382 --> 00:05:39.579
and they'll catch up eventually.

00:05:39.579 --> 00:05:43.067
That's what makes them a visionary.

00:05:43.067 --> 00:05:47.234
So I hope that the students
here at Dillard, they evolve.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:49.863
Hopefully you're growing
and you're learning.

00:05:49.863 --> 00:05:53.376
Not standing still and being stagnant.

00:05:53.376 --> 00:05:55.794
- What makes a good storyteller?

00:05:55.794 --> 00:05:57.123
- I don't know that's a--

00:05:57.123 --> 00:05:58.752
(Spike laughing)

00:05:58.752 --> 00:06:00.912
- [Marc] Right, trick question.

00:06:00.912 --> 00:06:02.862
- A fortune cookie.

00:06:02.862 --> 00:06:05.236
- But what from the patterns you've seen

00:06:05.236 --> 00:06:06.314
or things that you've applied?

00:06:06.314 --> 00:06:08.718
Were you just like one move, two moves,

00:06:08.718 --> 00:06:11.551
on the board that are going to assure

00:06:12.496 --> 00:06:15.391
a certain kind of qualitative refinement

00:06:15.391 --> 00:06:16.633
and the right storytelling.
- I think the storyteller

00:06:16.633 --> 00:06:20.321
has to believe in the story
that that person's telling

00:06:20.321 --> 00:06:21.648
'cause if you don't believe in it,

00:06:21.648 --> 00:06:22.891
the audience is not
going to believe in it.

00:06:22.891 --> 00:06:25.541
So you really have to believe

00:06:25.541 --> 00:06:27.330
that this is a good story.

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:28.819
I don't really want to
make any generalizations

00:06:28.819 --> 00:06:30.645
but a lot of people I've spoken to

00:06:30.645 --> 00:06:33.387
and I've spoken to some
great, great filmmakers

00:06:33.387 --> 00:06:34.220
and novelists.

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:35.803
They do stuff like.

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:38.730
I'll write a novel 'cause
it's the one I want to read.

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:40.096
- [Marc] Right.

00:06:40.096 --> 00:06:41.553
- This is a film I would want to see.

00:06:41.553 --> 00:06:42.721
- Right.

00:06:42.721 --> 00:06:44.500
- And hopefully.

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:45.379
- Yeah.

00:06:45.379 --> 00:06:48.363
- You hope and pray that the
audience will be in tune.

00:06:48.363 --> 00:06:49.905
- [Marc] Right.

00:06:49.905 --> 00:06:52.504
- I mean, one of the greatest
people with that gift,

00:06:52.504 --> 00:06:53.970
I think is Steven Spielberg

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:57.215
'cause he just has this intuition.

00:06:57.215 --> 00:06:58.048
- Yeah.

00:06:58.048 --> 00:06:59.326
- He knows, he could point out the pulse

00:06:59.326 --> 00:07:00.407
in what people want.

00:07:00.407 --> 00:07:02.848
- Right, that's a good anecdote.

00:07:02.848 --> 00:07:05.413
It's like if you're not believing in it,

00:07:05.413 --> 00:07:06.260
people see right through it.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:09.712
- You be faking the funk and
people are going to tell.

00:07:09.712 --> 00:07:10.867
Three years of film school,

00:07:10.867 --> 00:07:11.700
I do a film,

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:14.736
my thesis film is called
Joe's Bed-Stuy Barbershop

00:07:14.736 --> 00:07:17.605
and it wins the Student Academy Award.

00:07:17.605 --> 00:07:19.980
I won a Student Academy
Award back in 1983.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:21.510
(audience applauding)

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:23.010
So I had an agent.

00:07:24.423 --> 00:07:26.539
Man, I thought that Spielberg,

00:07:26.539 --> 00:07:30.778
Spielberg was going to call
me, Lucas, Fox, Universal.

00:07:30.778 --> 00:07:33.216
And I had a little
apartment off Myrtle Avenue

00:07:33.216 --> 00:07:36.160
on Fort Greene, before gentrification.

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:37.160
So I had my,

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:42.099
now the Student Academy
Award is not like this.

00:07:42.099 --> 00:07:46.099
It's a wooden box with a
circle with this on it.

00:07:48.163 --> 00:07:49.366
So I know for sure

00:07:49.366 --> 00:07:51.815
because I have a Student Academy Award,

00:07:51.815 --> 00:07:54.315
I'm going to take over Hollywood.

00:07:56.074 --> 00:07:58.241
But a funny thing happens.

00:07:59.893 --> 00:08:02.143
When you don't pay Ma Bell,

00:08:04.991 --> 00:08:06.979
you don't pay Con Edison

00:08:06.979 --> 00:08:10.261
and you don't pay Brooklyn Union Gas.

00:08:10.261 --> 00:08:12.633
The phone gets turned off,

00:08:12.633 --> 00:08:13.552
the heat gets turned off

00:08:13.552 --> 00:08:14.422
and lights get turned off.

00:08:14.422 --> 00:08:15.337
So I was in the dark

00:08:15.337 --> 00:08:17.205
just by having my Student Academy Award.

00:08:17.205 --> 00:08:18.981
(audience laughing)

00:08:18.981 --> 00:08:20.564
And then I woke up.

00:08:22.053 --> 00:08:26.374
And so that's when I
learned the tough lesson,

00:08:26.374 --> 00:08:27.786
nothing happens overnight.

00:08:27.786 --> 00:08:30.686
There's no such thing as overnight success

00:08:30.686 --> 00:08:32.840
and that's the biggest thing
we do to our young people.

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:36.173
Tell them that there's no thing or amble

00:08:37.112 --> 00:08:38.029
as overnight success.

00:08:38.029 --> 00:08:42.443
You have to bust your
ass, roll up your sleeves,

00:08:42.443 --> 00:08:46.610
and attack, attack, attack,
attack every single day.

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:48.841
And that's what I've been
telling my film students

00:08:48.841 --> 00:08:51.458
at NYU for the last 15 years.

00:08:51.458 --> 00:08:53.290
- How do you choose
what's interesting to you?

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:54.540
I mean how do you decide?

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:56.278
- It's something I have
to feel passionate about.

00:08:56.278 --> 00:08:59.024
I mean it, people don't know this

00:08:59.024 --> 00:09:03.191
but to make a good film has to
be one of the hardest things

00:09:05.228 --> 00:09:06.858
known to humankind to make.

00:09:06.858 --> 00:09:09.804
I mean there's so many factors

00:09:09.804 --> 00:09:12.914
that go into making a good film.

00:09:12.914 --> 00:09:17.081
And like filmmakers who
are passionate about this,

00:09:18.945 --> 00:09:20.812
we kill ourselves to do this.

00:09:20.812 --> 00:09:23.339
So you spend a year, two years,

00:09:23.339 --> 00:09:26.408
and Malcolm X, that took two years.

00:09:26.408 --> 00:09:28.066
That's time out of your life

00:09:28.066 --> 00:09:30.346
and you have to have that passion

00:09:30.346 --> 00:09:32.087
for the subject matter, for the story.

00:09:32.087 --> 00:09:36.943
Whatever it is that makes you
want to go ahead and persevere.

00:09:36.943 --> 00:09:41.842
I'll give the same advice to
aspiring filmmaker and writer.

00:09:41.842 --> 00:09:42.675
Write.

00:09:43.658 --> 00:09:47.575
Both with those disciplines,
you need to write.

00:09:50.256 --> 00:09:52.133
And that's something I
tell my film students.

00:09:52.133 --> 00:09:53.377
I've been teaching,

00:09:53.377 --> 00:09:56.786
I've been a professor at NYU last 15 years

00:09:56.786 --> 00:09:59.335
in the graduate film school where I went.

00:09:59.335 --> 00:10:00.758
And if you did a survey,

00:10:00.758 --> 00:10:02.258
the last 25 years,

00:10:03.537 --> 00:10:07.120
and looked at the first
films of directors,

00:10:09.401 --> 00:10:13.025
I would say a large
percentage of those films,

00:10:13.025 --> 00:10:15.858
those directors wrote that script.

00:10:17.269 --> 00:10:21.348
So I think that's a big clue
when I'm talking about film,

00:10:21.348 --> 00:10:25.515
that if you want to be a director
and you could write also,

00:10:27.199 --> 00:10:29.201
you have a much better chance

00:10:29.201 --> 00:10:31.151
of getting your first film made.

00:10:31.151 --> 00:10:32.198
- Do the Right Thing.

00:10:32.198 --> 00:10:34.103
That's what many are saying to Spike Lee

00:10:34.103 --> 00:10:35.945
as they call on the famed director

00:10:35.945 --> 00:10:38.773
to end his new Kickstarter campaign.

00:10:38.773 --> 00:10:39.606
Now if you don't know,

00:10:39.606 --> 00:10:40.763
Kickstarter's a website that helps--

00:10:40.763 --> 00:10:41.596
- [Spike] Wait, wait.

00:10:41.596 --> 00:10:42.474
- [Trish] Hang on, hang on,

00:10:42.474 --> 00:10:44.028
we're going to go there.

00:10:44.028 --> 00:10:45.817
- Why did you open like that?

00:10:45.817 --> 00:10:46.987
Why will you open like that?

00:10:46.987 --> 00:10:49.189
- Because we're all
about controversy here.

00:10:49.189 --> 00:10:50.476
We're going to let you answer in a second

00:10:50.476 --> 00:10:53.054
but I got to tell people what
the Kickstarter thing is.

00:10:53.054 --> 00:10:53.887
It's a website.

00:10:53.887 --> 00:10:54.720
- [Spike] Why would you open like that?

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:57.934
- That helps artist fund their
projects using crowdsourcing.

00:10:57.934 --> 00:10:58.767
Here's how it works,

00:10:58.767 --> 00:10:59.600
you make your pitch in a video,

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:01.681
you upload it, you hope
the idea takes off,

00:11:01.681 --> 00:11:04.083
it's mostly for up-and-coming novices.

00:11:04.083 --> 00:11:05.958
So what is an Oscar-nominated

00:11:05.958 --> 00:11:09.408
director like Spike Lee doing on this set?

00:11:09.408 --> 00:11:11.442
We thought we'd ask him in person.

00:11:11.442 --> 00:11:14.673
- Well, I would like
to refute your opening.

00:11:14.673 --> 00:11:15.506
- Come on just tell me,

00:11:15.506 --> 00:11:17.335
I mean you've got a lot of money.

00:11:17.335 --> 00:11:18.168
You don't need to do this.

00:11:18.168 --> 00:11:19.001
- First of all, first
of all, first of all--

00:11:19.001 --> 00:11:20.507
- Why would you go to Kickstarter?

00:11:20.507 --> 00:11:21.504
- First of all--
- I think it's interesting

00:11:21.504 --> 00:11:22.337
and fascinating.

00:11:22.337 --> 00:11:25.806
- First of all, you don't
know how much money I have.

00:11:25.806 --> 00:11:27.011
You've never even seen me before,

00:11:27.011 --> 00:11:28.385
you've never seen me before in your life.

00:11:28.385 --> 00:11:29.928
So for you to assume

00:11:29.928 --> 00:11:33.713
what my financial state is, that's wrong.

00:11:33.713 --> 00:11:35.208
Second of all,

00:11:35.208 --> 00:11:38.159
I've been doing Kickstarter
before there was Kickstarter.

00:11:38.159 --> 00:11:39.120
- [Trish] So you know

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:39.953
how to raise money?
- Let me, can I finish?

00:11:39.953 --> 00:11:42.411
Can I finish, please?

00:11:42.411 --> 00:11:43.369
I only have six minutes

00:11:43.369 --> 00:11:45.941
so I would like to get my voice heard.

00:11:45.941 --> 00:11:49.637
I've been doing Kickstarter
before there was Kickstarter.

00:11:49.637 --> 00:11:52.339
That's how I raised my first
film, She's Got to Have It.

00:11:52.339 --> 00:11:53.582
- [Trish] So why go to Kickstarter then?

00:11:53.582 --> 00:11:54.607
If you know how to raise money,

00:11:54.607 --> 00:11:55.684
why are you going there?

00:11:55.684 --> 00:12:00.561
- Because crowdfunding is the
new wave to get financing.

00:12:00.561 --> 00:12:04.667
Crowdfunding, where you
go directly to your base,

00:12:04.667 --> 00:12:07.570
directly to the people who
love She's Got to Have It,

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:11.699
School Daze, Do the
Right Thing, Malcolm X,

00:12:11.699 --> 00:12:15.076
He Got Game, Summer of Sam, 25th Hour,

00:12:15.076 --> 00:12:18.909
Inside Man, Kings of
Comedy, Kobe Doin' Work.

00:12:20.286 --> 00:12:23.113
That's a body of work that's been amassed

00:12:23.113 --> 00:12:27.280
over three decades and I'm
going directly to the people.

00:12:29.041 --> 00:12:30.185
No, no, no, no.

00:12:30.185 --> 00:12:31.335
- [Trish] I want to know what's different

00:12:31.335 --> 00:12:32.185
about then and now.

00:12:32.185 --> 00:12:33.053
So you've done it before.

00:12:33.053 --> 00:12:34.376
- What's different is that

00:12:34.376 --> 00:12:38.145
the whole Hollywood game has changed.

00:12:38.145 --> 00:12:42.307
Now studios only want
to make tent-pole films

00:12:42.307 --> 00:12:45.138
that can open on the same day globally

00:12:45.138 --> 00:12:47.006
and make trillions of dollars.

00:12:47.006 --> 00:12:49.012
- Which by the way aren't
doing well this year are they?

00:12:49.012 --> 00:12:50.555
- Exactly, that's the same thing

00:12:50.555 --> 00:12:53.504
why Spielberg and George Lucas themselves,

00:12:53.504 --> 00:12:57.114
who you might say created
the blockbuster phenomena

00:12:57.114 --> 00:13:00.222
said that this path of tent-poles

00:13:00.222 --> 00:13:03.196
is going to collapse the
whole Hollywood industry

00:13:03.196 --> 00:13:04.156
and that's not me.

00:13:04.156 --> 00:13:06.593
That's Spielberg and
Lucas are saying that.

00:13:06.593 --> 00:13:09.291
So therefore what I want to do.

00:13:09.291 --> 00:13:11.296
My new film coming out

00:13:11.296 --> 00:13:14.150
Thanksgiving weekend is Old
Boy, that's a studio film.

00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:16.972
Josh Brolin and Sam Jackson.

00:13:16.972 --> 00:13:20.917
This is a film that is
an independent film.

00:13:20.917 --> 00:13:22.751
I'm an independent filmmaker.

00:13:22.751 --> 00:13:24.668
So I'm appealing to my,

00:13:25.652 --> 00:13:29.485
I'm appealing to my base
go to Kickstarter.com

00:13:30.386 --> 00:13:32.982
to pledge as little as $5
to help me get my new film--

00:13:32.982 --> 00:13:34.674
- [Trish] Hey, you're almost there right?

00:13:34.674 --> 00:13:35.507
You're a third

00:13:35.507 --> 00:13:37.586
of the way there?
- To get my new film made.

00:13:37.586 --> 00:13:39.491
- [Trish] You need one
and a quarter million?

00:13:39.491 --> 00:13:40.325
- Yes.

00:13:40.325 --> 00:13:42.523
- [Trish] And you're the
third of the way there.

00:13:42.523 --> 00:13:43.848
- And we're 'na make it.

00:13:43.848 --> 00:13:47.209
Much to your chagrin,
we will make it,people.

00:13:47.209 --> 00:13:48.878
Hey, don't worry about her.

00:13:48.878 --> 00:13:51.616
- Hey, I'm all for you making it, Spike.

00:13:51.616 --> 00:13:52.946
- Don't worry about haters like her,

00:13:52.946 --> 00:13:53.983
we're going to get there.

00:13:53.983 --> 00:13:55.647
- I'm all for you making this film.

00:13:55.647 --> 00:13:57.599
I'm trying to understand why.

00:13:57.599 --> 00:14:00.128
- You cannot be for my film

00:14:00.128 --> 00:14:02.165
when you open up a segment like that.

00:14:02.165 --> 00:14:04.204
- A very interesting thing.
- That is pure hate.

00:14:04.204 --> 00:14:05.366
That was pure hate.

00:14:05.366 --> 00:14:08.276
I'm sorry Trish but
I'll tell you something.

00:14:08.276 --> 00:14:09.233
Hate conquers love.

00:14:09.233 --> 00:14:12.096
- [Trish] These are the concerns
of some people out there.

00:14:12.096 --> 00:14:13.268
Spike, that is my job.

00:14:13.268 --> 00:14:14.472
This is not my concern.

00:14:14.472 --> 00:14:15.472
It's the concern

00:14:15.472 --> 00:14:16.757
of the people out there.
- People had the concern

00:14:16.757 --> 00:14:18.253
that when Do the Right Thing released,

00:14:18.253 --> 00:14:20.368
black people run amok and riot.

00:14:20.368 --> 00:14:21.744
So what does that mean?

00:14:21.744 --> 00:14:23.372
We all have concerns.
- I am voicing the concern

00:14:23.372 --> 00:14:24.740
of what some have said.

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:25.816
- Some.
- Okay, you got to be able

00:14:25.816 --> 00:14:26.992
to take the criticism, right?

00:14:26.992 --> 00:14:28.239
- Oh, I'm taking it.

00:14:28.239 --> 00:14:30.892
But I'm not going to sit here

00:14:30.892 --> 00:14:35.059
and just let you say that
stuff without me refuting it.

00:14:36.779 --> 00:14:38.565
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:14:38.565 --> 00:14:41.595
I made this video because
Fredricka Fonta asked me to.

00:14:41.595 --> 00:14:42.722
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:14:42.722 --> 00:14:44.423
that you want me to profile next,

00:14:44.423 --> 00:14:46.737
leave it down the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:14:46.737 --> 00:14:48.727
I'd also love to know
which of Spike's rules

00:14:48.727 --> 00:14:49.846
had the biggest impact on you?

00:14:49.846 --> 00:14:51.637
What did he say that was really memorable

00:14:51.637 --> 00:14:52.882
and meaningful to you?

00:14:52.882 --> 00:14:53.715
Leave it in the comments.

00:14:53.715 --> 00:14:55.203
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:14:55.203 --> 00:14:57.653
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Stacey Crewe.

00:14:57.653 --> 00:14:59.555
Stacey, thank you so much
for picking up a copy

00:14:59.555 --> 00:15:01.135
of my book, Your One Word.

00:15:01.135 --> 00:15:02.875
It really, really,
really means a lot to me.

00:15:02.875 --> 00:15:04.534
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:15:04.534 --> 00:15:05.709
I believe in you.

00:15:05.709 --> 00:15:08.113
I hope you continue to
believe in yourselves.

00:15:08.113 --> 00:15:11.230
And whatever your one word is, much love.

00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:12.730
I'll see you soon.

00:15:18.902 --> 00:15:22.555
- I've been very fortunate to make,

00:15:22.555 --> 00:15:27.163
the average output of film a
year 86, we made two this year.

00:15:27.163 --> 00:15:29.509
But we're very happy about that

00:15:29.509 --> 00:15:31.914
because I think that's the only true way

00:15:31.914 --> 00:15:34.664
you're able to intelligently put,

00:15:38.629 --> 00:15:40.206
I mean evaluate an artist's work.

00:15:40.206 --> 00:15:43.776
So often today, somebody does one movie,

00:15:43.776 --> 00:15:46.193
one album, one play, one book

00:15:47.486 --> 00:15:49.972
and then they're a genius.

00:15:49.972 --> 00:15:52.472
And it doesn't work like that.

00:15:53.374 --> 00:15:56.107
You have to put together.

00:15:56.107 --> 00:15:57.179
You have to be consistent,

00:15:57.179 --> 00:15:58.567
you have to put together a body of work

00:15:58.567 --> 00:16:01.355
and that's how you evaluate artists.

00:16:01.355 --> 00:16:04.224
I mean somebody comes into
the NBA and have a good year.

00:16:04.224 --> 00:16:06.128
They say this guy's the
next Michael Jordan.

00:16:06.128 --> 00:16:07.876
You know that's bull.

00:16:07.876 --> 00:16:10.744
If you could do what Michael Jordan's done

00:16:10.744 --> 00:16:12.484
for 10, nine years,

00:16:12.484 --> 00:16:14.472
every year at the top of this game,

00:16:14.472 --> 00:16:18.208
then you're able to say
to compare somebody.

00:16:18.208 --> 00:16:20.114
They've only been in the league one year?

00:16:20.114 --> 00:16:21.486
How could you make that comparison?

00:16:21.486 --> 00:16:22.851
You just can't do it.

00:16:22.851 --> 00:16:25.507
- What advice would you
give to artists of color,

00:16:25.507 --> 00:16:27.331
LGBT artists, women artists,

00:16:27.331 --> 00:16:29.787
artists who are outside
of the dominant culture?

00:16:29.787 --> 00:16:32.460
What would you tell us on how to nurture

00:16:32.460 --> 00:16:34.573
and nourish ourselves
so that we have careers

00:16:34.573 --> 00:16:38.273
as long and as varied and
as beautiful as yours?

00:16:38.273 --> 00:16:39.106
- Is it a different reality

00:16:39.106 --> 00:16:40.936
for somebody who's making
a film in the margins

00:16:40.936 --> 00:16:44.104
as opposed to what she
said, the dominant culture?

00:16:44.104 --> 00:16:46.311
- Yeah, but you can't let that stop you.

00:16:46.311 --> 00:16:47.973
When I was in film school, there was only,

00:16:47.973 --> 00:16:51.959
when I was in NYU film
school from 1979 to 1982,

00:16:51.959 --> 00:16:53.908
there was only one
African-American director

00:16:53.908 --> 00:16:55.558
working in Hollywood, period.

00:16:55.558 --> 00:16:56.558
His name is,

00:16:59.364 --> 00:17:01.308
Michael Schultz, excuse me,

00:17:01.308 --> 00:17:02.641
and at the time,

00:17:03.578 --> 00:17:06.288
he was directing Richard Pryor's films.

00:17:06.288 --> 00:17:09.152
Richard Pryor was the
biggest star in Hollywood.

00:17:09.152 --> 00:17:10.936
So if I'm in film school
and says the only one,

00:17:10.936 --> 00:17:14.921
I mean if I'd had
thought about those odds,

00:17:14.921 --> 00:17:16.255
it would have hindered me.

00:17:16.255 --> 00:17:19.691
- So what do you make of Colin
Kaepernick not standing up?

00:17:19.691 --> 00:17:21.020
- I support him.

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:25.837
I find it so interesting how
people want to pick and choose

00:17:25.837 --> 00:17:27.538
what rights people have.

00:17:27.538 --> 00:17:31.319
Now any time you talk
about anti-gun violence,

00:17:31.319 --> 00:17:33.107
people run around screaming about

00:17:33.107 --> 00:17:35.973
they don't want their
Second Amendment rights

00:17:35.973 --> 00:17:37.262
to be infringed upon.

00:17:37.262 --> 00:17:40.679
The same way John Carlos and Tommie Smith

00:17:42.118 --> 00:17:43.738
raised their black-gloved fist

00:17:43.738 --> 00:17:45.748
at the '68 Olympics in Mexico,

00:17:45.748 --> 00:17:49.915
the same way Muhammad Ali
refused to fight for injustice,

00:17:51.734 --> 00:17:53.900
a war that was crazy.

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:54.733
- [Anderson] Refused to
fight the Vietnam War.

00:17:54.733 --> 00:17:56.882
- Yes, the Vietnam War.

00:17:56.882 --> 00:18:00.616
These are rights that Americans have.

00:18:00.616 --> 00:18:03.000
- You see, you see what he is doing,

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:05.457
what Colin Kaepernick is
doing is in that tradition.

00:18:05.457 --> 00:18:07.034
- Yes, it's in that tradition

00:18:07.034 --> 00:18:11.890
of black athletes standing
up, using their platform,

00:18:11.890 --> 00:18:14.640
saying I'm not happy with the way

00:18:16.814 --> 00:18:18.675
black people, people of
color are being treated.

00:18:18.675 --> 00:18:21.093
- It's so interesting because often

00:18:21.093 --> 00:18:24.637
at the time when an athlete
does it, they are vilified.

00:18:24.637 --> 00:18:26.690
- Oh that's the thing.
- And years later,

00:18:26.690 --> 00:18:27.651
I mean Muhammad Ali.

00:18:27.651 --> 00:18:31.312
- The biggest revisionist story ever,

00:18:31.312 --> 00:18:33.261
Muhammad Ali one time was the most

00:18:33.261 --> 00:18:34.094
hated person.
- Hated.

00:18:34.094 --> 00:18:35.137
Hated, yeah.

00:18:35.137 --> 00:18:36.546
- People need to Google.

00:18:36.546 --> 00:18:37.502
Do research.

00:18:37.502 --> 00:18:41.924
Muhammad Ali was the most
hated person in America.

00:18:41.924 --> 00:18:43.166
- [Anderson] He couldn't box in America.

00:18:43.166 --> 00:18:44.788
- Yeah, the Muhammad Ali people love is,

00:18:44.788 --> 00:18:49.193
him lighting the torch
where his arm is shaking

00:18:49.193 --> 00:18:52.098
in the Olympics in Atlanta.

00:18:52.098 --> 00:18:53.765
But not the defiant.

00:18:54.752 --> 00:18:56.623
He said no Viet Cong ever called him,

00:18:56.623 --> 00:18:59.279
I'm not going to say the N-word.

00:18:59.279 --> 00:19:00.903
And he sacrificed the prime.

00:19:00.903 --> 00:19:03.149
- New York Times would not use his name.

00:19:03.149 --> 00:19:05.098
They called him Cassius Clay.

00:19:05.098 --> 00:19:06.711
- Now you call the guy's name.

00:19:06.711 --> 00:19:09.194
So I think this is in the same tradition.

00:19:09.194 --> 00:19:10.392
What I find, hold on, I like to this.

00:19:10.392 --> 00:19:11.225
- [Anderson] Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:19:11.225 --> 00:19:14.056
- I find it very interesting

00:19:14.056 --> 00:19:17.044
that three members of Big Blue,

00:19:17.044 --> 00:19:19.461
the New York Football Giants,

00:19:20.368 --> 00:19:23.451
one presently playing and two retired

00:19:24.893 --> 00:19:27.564
are the ones that criticized him.

00:19:27.564 --> 00:19:31.231
Three are the New,
that's my team, football.

00:19:32.219 --> 00:19:33.337
Three of those brothers,

00:19:33.337 --> 00:19:35.333
they should, I'm really, I'm not,

00:19:35.333 --> 00:19:39.554
they should understand in what tradition,

00:19:39.554 --> 00:19:40.471
the history

00:19:41.881 --> 00:19:43.714
of why Colin did this.

00:19:45.864 --> 00:19:46.997
Now I bet if you went
to all three of them.

00:19:46.997 --> 00:19:48.698
They would say they love Muhammad Ali.

00:19:48.698 --> 00:19:49.953
How could you love Muhammad Ali

00:19:49.953 --> 00:19:52.815
and not love him for the stance he took

00:19:52.815 --> 00:19:55.507
not to be inducted in the Vietnam War?

00:19:55.507 --> 00:19:59.194
If you say you like
Tommie Smith, John Carlos,

00:19:59.194 --> 00:20:00.687
yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:20:00.687 --> 00:20:01.766
It's the same thing.

00:20:01.766 --> 00:20:02.677
- [Anderson] Muhammad Ali said,

00:20:02.677 --> 00:20:04.177
"If I kept my mouth shut

00:20:04.177 --> 00:20:05.675
"just because I can make millions

00:20:05.675 --> 00:20:07.008
"then this ain't doing nothing.

00:20:07.008 --> 00:20:09.127
"So I just love the freedom
and the flesh and blood

00:20:09.127 --> 00:20:11.825
"of my people more so
than I do the money."

00:20:11.825 --> 00:20:13.280
And Jackie Robinson said,

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:15.152
"I cannot stand and sing the anthem

00:20:15.152 --> 00:20:16.774
"I cannot salute the flag,

00:20:16.774 --> 00:20:18.598
"I know that I'm a black
man in a white world."

00:20:18.598 --> 00:20:21.923
That's Jackie Robinson in
1972 in his autobiography.

00:20:21.923 --> 00:20:23.448
It is so interesting how and I--

00:20:23.448 --> 00:20:25.318
- This is the tradition.

00:20:25.318 --> 00:20:27.818
What Colin is doing now
has not just come out,

00:20:27.818 --> 00:20:29.145
pop out of nowhere.

00:20:29.145 --> 00:20:30.184
There's tradition.

00:20:30.184 --> 00:20:33.096
Tradition of African-American athletes

00:20:33.096 --> 00:20:35.549
standing up and saying,
using their platform

00:20:35.549 --> 00:20:37.800
to say something is wrong.

00:20:37.800 --> 00:20:39.091
- Do you think he's going
to pay a price for it?

00:20:39.091 --> 00:20:39.924
I mean--

00:20:39.924 --> 00:20:41.554
- They all do.

00:20:41.554 --> 00:20:45.514
Muhammad Ali, three years of his prime.

00:20:45.514 --> 00:20:48.551
John Carlos, Tommie Smith, of course.

00:20:48.551 --> 00:20:51.384
But these brothers do this knowing

00:20:52.701 --> 00:20:55.911
that there will be ramifications
and they don't care

00:20:55.911 --> 00:20:58.403
because this is their belief.

00:20:58.403 --> 00:20:59.315
- It's a--

00:20:59.315 --> 00:21:01.080
- And I'm about to say this.

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:02.190
They always bring up,

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:04.397
oh, you're making a lot of money.

00:21:04.397 --> 00:21:06.390
So the reason because you make money

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:10.557
that means that you can't have
a moral foundation and speak.

00:21:12.123 --> 00:21:13.693
I mean like, 'cause here's the thing,

00:21:13.693 --> 00:21:16.514
black athletes, we pay you money.

00:21:16.514 --> 00:21:19.988
Play ball, shut up, don't say nothing.

00:21:19.988 --> 00:21:20.859
We're giving you money.

00:21:20.859 --> 00:21:23.015
Million dollars, just go out there,

00:21:23.015 --> 00:21:24.678
run up and down the gridiron.

00:21:24.678 --> 00:21:25.511
- [Anderson] I mean there
have been a lot of athletes--

00:21:25.511 --> 00:21:27.667
- Run up and down the court
and just be quiet and play.

00:21:27.667 --> 00:21:28.701
- There have been a lot of athletes

00:21:28.701 --> 00:21:31.972
who don't want to risk
their endorsement deals,

00:21:31.972 --> 00:21:32.805
things like that.

00:21:32.805 --> 00:21:33.638
- Look, I understand that.

00:21:33.638 --> 00:21:36.822
It's all an individual choice

00:21:36.822 --> 00:21:40.572
but when someone has
the courage to step out,

00:21:42.403 --> 00:21:44.276
with knowing they can lose all that,

00:21:44.276 --> 00:21:46.144
why are you going to
jump on the brother, man?

00:21:46.144 --> 00:21:47.610
- Do you stand up during
the national anthem?

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:48.563
- Yes.

00:21:48.563 --> 00:21:50.813
But it's a personal choice.

00:21:53.774 --> 00:21:55.099
That is not.

00:21:55.099 --> 00:21:57.545
Me standing up for the national anthem

00:21:57.545 --> 00:22:01.088
does not affect me doing
Malcolm X or Do the Right Thing

00:22:01.088 --> 00:22:02.620
and all the other documentaries.

00:22:02.620 --> 00:22:05.484
So that does not do it for me.

00:22:05.484 --> 00:22:08.839
I stand up but I'm not going to blast.

00:22:08.839 --> 00:22:09.773
- You also stand up for his right not to?

00:22:09.773 --> 00:22:10.731
- [Spike] Yes.

